Step 5 of 1000. Reading src/invoices.ts, I wrote down the four callers of totalsFor and what each one does with the result.

```ts
function summarize(invoice: Invoice): InvoiceSummary {
  const total = invoiceTotal(invoice);
  const due = dueDate(invoice);
  return {
    id: invoice.id,
    totalCents: total,
    due,
    overdue: due < todayISO(),
  };
}
```

Step 10 of 1000. I went through src/invoices.ts line by line and marked the two spots where the total is rounded.

```ts
export function parseAmount(raw: string): number {
  const value = Number(raw.replace(/[^0-9.-]/g, ""));
  if (!Number.isFinite(value)) {
    throw new RangeError("not an amount: " + raw);
  }
  return Math.round(value * 100);
}
```

Step 15 of 1000. I read src/invoices.ts and counted five places that build an invoice total, three of them in tests.

```ts
function parseInvoice(row: Record<string, string>): Invoice | null {
  const id = row.id;
  if (!id) return null;
  const issued = row.issued ?? todayISO();
  const netDays = Number(row.netDays ?? "30");
  const items = row.items === undefined ? [] : JSON.parse(row.items);
  return { id, issued, netDays, items };
}
```

